\description{
Companion plot function for \code{\link[=run_slopes_vs_zero]{run_slopes_vs_zero()}} and
\code{\link[=run_gated_slopes]{run_gated_slopes()}}. For every \strong{significant row} in the input
results table, draws one figure showing the fitted slope line(s)
involved in that specific test, with an optional CI ribbon and
optional raw-data overlay. Designed for the "categorical by
continuous" side of second-level analyses — the discrete counterpart
to \code{\link[=plot_simple_slopes]{plot_simple_slopes()}}.

Two input shapes are supported and auto-detected:
\itemize{
\item \strong{Activation input} — output of \code{\link[=run_slopes_vs_zero]{run_slopes_vs_zero()}}. Each
significant row is plotted as a single slope line on the
continuous moderator (\code{trend_var}), optionally filtered to the
level named in the row's \code{predictor} column.
\item \strong{Gated post-hoc input} — output of \code{\link[=run_gated_slopes]{run_gated_slopes()}}. Each
significant row is plotted as the two slope lines involved in the
pairwise contrast recorded in that row (e.g., Eval vs No Eval at
\code{cond = plan} for a three-way Group:cond:trend_var effect).
}

Mode is detected automatically from the presence of the
\code{posthoc_type} column. Per-row (not per-cell) plotting keeps each
figure focused on exactly the effect that made the row significant
— no extra lines that were not part of the test.
}
\details{
\strong{Three-way decomposition.} For rows with
\code{posthoc_type = "<A>By<B>"} (a three-way interaction decomposed as
"contrast A within each level of B"), the plot shows two lines —
the two levels of A named in the row's \code{contrast} column — both at
the specific level of B recorded implicitly in the row's \code{predictor}
column. The conditioning level is parsed out of the \code{predictor}
value by stripping the contrast text and the \code{suffix}. Example:
\code{predictor = "plan_Eval - No Eval_CortSlopeDiff"},
\code{contrast = "Eval - No Eval"}, \code{suffix = "CortSlopeDiff"} →
conditioning level \code{"plan"}.

\strong{Raw-data overlay.} When \code{show_points = TRUE} (default), the
subject-level data points belonging to the group(s) being plotted
are overlaid on the lines — filtered to match the level of each
line. For activation plots that means subjects matching the row's
\code{predictor} level (e.g., only Eval subjects on the "Eval slope"
plot). For two-way and three-way post-hoc plots that means subjects
matching the contrast level (and conditioning level, for three-way).
Outliers and sample size become visible; the slope CIs are
less easy to take on faith.

\strong{CI ribbon.} When \code{show_ci = TRUE} (default), a ribbon
around each line shows the pointwise 95\% CI for the predicted
response, obtained from \code{emmeans::emmeans(fit, ..., at = list(grid))}
with \code{infer = c(TRUE, TRUE)}. For mixed models this uses the
Kenward-Roger / Satterthwaite machinery emmeans is already
configured with; for \code{lm} + HC\* the CI uses the robust covariance
passed as \code{vcov.}.
}
